-\textbf{Existing defenses on Protocol B.} The comparisons above use the Protocol~A slice and different metrics per system. To compare end-to-end outcomes directly, we ran four existing defenses on the 200 planted attacks and 200 benign questions of Protocol~B, with the same single generator, decoding, and scoring as Table~\ref{tab:ablation}, paired row by row (Table~\ref{tab:existing}). \emph{Spotlighting}~\cite{hines2024spotlighting} uses its datamarking variant: each retrieved passage has a marker interleaved between its words and the system prompt explains the marker; the answers are regenerated. \emph{LLM-Guard}~\cite{protectai2024} is used as an input firewall: its prompt-injection scanner (threshold 0.5) checks the query and every passage placed in the prompt, and any flag refuses the query. \emph{ControlNET} is the detector described above (Llama-3.1-8B layer-1 activations, decision tree), retrained on the same data and given exactly the eight reranked passages the undefended generator received (the planted chunk survives reranking in 184 of the 200 rows). LLM-Guard and ControlNET only block or pass, so a guarded deployment delivers the undefended answer on every row they pass; we score them by applying their decisions to the stored undefended answers, after recomputing the passages the undefended pipeline retrieved (retrieval is deterministic). The full stack lets 3.5\% of attacks succeed, against 21.0\% for LLM-Guard (3 rows succeed only under the full stack, 38 only under LLM-Guard; $p=1\times10^{-8}$) and 23.0\% for Spotlighting ($p<10^{-9}$), with no significant difference in benign correctness from either. Spotlighting refuses no benign question but stops few attacks. ControlNET is the strongest existing defense: its 4.5\% ASR is statistically indistinguishable from the full stack's ($p=0.77$) and from the three-layer subset's 5.5\% ($p=0.80$). It also blocks 15 of the 16 attacks whose planted chunk never reached the generator, so much of its signal comes from the coercive wording of the attack queries rather than from the planted passage. An earlier variant that appended the planted chunk to every row gave 5.0\% ASR with the same conclusions. It answers fewer benign questions correctly (46.0\% vs.\ 52.0\%; $p=0.058$ against the full stack and $p=0.012$ against the three-layer subset) and refuses more (13.5\% vs.\ 8.5\%, $p=0.13$). The defensible claim is therefore that the seven-layer configuration clearly outperforms an input firewall and prompt-level spotlighting, and matches the strongest detector we tested on attack success with somewhat better benign utility; it does not establish superiority over ControlNET. Two caveats apply. Our ControlNET retraining reaches a validation F1 of 0.88 against 0.90 in the run reported above (a newer \texttt{transformers} release was used there), and ControlNET's mitigation module (ProNet) is not reimplemented, so it is evaluated as a detect-and-block firewall. \emph{ADO}~\cite{palleria2026ado} runs from the authors' unmodified code through an adapter: for each query its Sentinel and Strategist (Llama-3.1-8B via Ollama) choose among DP-RAG, TrustRAG, and attention-variance filtering, these defenses act on the same eight passages, and the same generator then answers with ADO's own prompt. Local hardware forced two substitutions (Llama-3.1-8B instead of Llama-3-8B as controller, and a 4-bit Llama-3.1-8B for the attention filter), and every query starts as a fresh user, so ADO's trust mechanism is inactive; we therefore call it an adapted implementation, not a reproduction. ADO lets 28.5\% of attacks succeed, against 3.5\% for the full stack (54 vs.\ 4 discordant rows, $p=3\times10^{-12}$), and answers fewer benign questions correctly (35.5\% vs.\ 52.0\%, $p=6\times10^{-6}$). Both effects have one main cause: DP-RAG removes every passage on 64 of the 200 attacks and 58 of the 200 benign questions. The generator then refuses (``No relevant information was found\ldots''), which stops the attack but also discards the evidence, and which the marker scorer counts as a success whenever the planted answer is ``No'' (30 of ADO's 57 successes). If refusals are not counted as attack successes for any arm, ADO's ASR is 13.5\% against 3.5\% (24 vs.\ 4 discordant rows, $p=2\times10^{-4}$); this criterion was preregistered for 150 of the 200 attacks and fixed after the pilot for the other 50. These results concern this adapted implementation under Protocol~B's marker scoring; they do not establish superiority over every faithful ADO deployment. Its median latency is 45\,s per query, against 15.3\,s for the full stack.
